  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1217 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nShortwave trough was ejecting into the central plains at midday\nas expected, with the trailing trough axis bisecting SW KS.\nLate morning radar showed the final stratiform rain band\nweakening and exiting the eastern zones, and the majority of the\nrain event has ended. Rainfall amounts from this event have been\ncommonly 0.5 - 1 inch, with highest amounts near Hugoton, near \n2 inches. Additional rainfall over the northeast panhandles and\nnorthwest Oklahoma will enter the southeast zones through this\nafternoon. Models suggest additional scattered showers will\nredevelop this afternoon into tonight, as final trailing\nshortwaves arrive, and before the primary trough axis ejects\neastward. This activity will be much more scattered in nature\nversus the stratiform rain of the previous 2 days. As such, NBM\npops for tonight seem too wet, but were accepted in the likely\nto definite category. Instablity has been weak to absent through\nthis event, and will continue to be the case, as such thunder\nwill be limited to absent through tonight. Additional rainfall\nthrough Tuesday morning will favor areas southeast of Dodge\nCity. Minimum temperatures sunrise Tuesday morning will remain\nwell above normal, mid 40s north to mid 50s southeast. \n\nWidespread stratus Tuesday morning is expected to dissolve\nthrough the day, with subsidence clearing the sky and ending all\nrainfall. Models show modest warming of the lower atmosphere\nTuesday, and along with improved southerly mixing and more\nsunshine, temperatures will warm several degrees into the upper\n60s to lower 70s. Stronger warming is expected Wednesday, with\nafternoon temperatures well into the upper 70s to near 80 - \n10 to 15 degrees above late October normals. Wednesday and\nThursday will both remain dry, with continued well above normal\nafternoon temperatures in the 70s. \n\nFriday, a strong 540 dm closed cyclone is expected to remain\nstationary over the Pacific Northwest. In response, broad SWly\nmidlevel flow will be maintained across the Rockies and over the\nplains, ensuring a continuation of southerly surface flow and \nmild temperatures. Models maintain continuity from previous runs\nsuggesting a tropical moisture source (some of it remnant \nmoisture from Pacific tropical cyclone Vance) will become \nentrained in the SW midlevel flow, with showers and \nthunderstorms returning to SW KS on Friday. Pops and rainfall \namounts will favor the southeast zones as the current NBM pop \nalignment in the grids suggests. \n\nUnseasonably warm southerly flow will continue through Sunday,\nand into November, with both afternoon and sunrise temperatures\nwell above normal. Models hint a strong trough and associated\ncold front passage around Monday November 2nd. Winds may\napproach high wind criteria with this system, but it is much \ntoo soon for any details.",
